#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222338} :activity/id #uuid "799f17ca-e712-547a-8b6c-91fc224e0a3d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050611 :topic/id #uuid "58ba8166-5e45-548c-9143-2c0d455f1d66" :topic/math-academy-id 6400} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222338 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222489 :tutorial/content "Can we apply Euler's method to approximate solutions to second-order initial value problems, such as the one below?\n\n$$\nxy'' - y' - y = 2x, \\qquad y(1) = -1, \\quad y'(1) = 1\n$$\n\nWell, for a second-order initial value problem,\n\n$$\ny'' = f(x, y, y'), \\qquad y(a) = c, \\quad y'(a) = d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{'}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx \\\\ Δz={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and $z_\\text{new} = z + \\Delta z.$\n\nIn the example above, we set up Euler's method by first isolating $y''$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{''}-{y}^{'}-y & =2x \\\\ x{y}^{''} & =y+{y}^{'}+2x \\\\ {y}^{''} & =\\frac{y+{y}^{'}}{x}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z=y',$ $z'=y''$ in the above. This gives the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{'}=\\frac{y+z}{x}+2,\\, & z(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the next slide, we will approximate values for this system using step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6d9782a2-388c-59cd-bb4e-165619f75de3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14485 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cbcb5ea9-acb5-53ee-a98c-a6a1f2ee7329" :step/math-academy-id 594421 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222396}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222339 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221423 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221173 :question/id #uuid "81a8347f-7852-5413-8d96-946ec658929e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21664"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55d2b186-64d2-5ad8-b168-6c7bea413df3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050629}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308460} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308462} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365633}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Row of a Table Using Euler's Method for Second-Order ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "ba8deacf-a74a-5730-a2a4-637d1cc352aa" :step/math-academy-id 594422 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222340}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222340 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221424 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221176 :question/id #uuid "62004d73-b9ca-5133-b016-e2694a55211c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21668"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd3b7856-4ac5-5473-a585-949dd06100e8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308512} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365645}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With One Step"} :step/id #uuid "c9df785c-d2e4-5fb4-ab71-6b247003510d" :step/math-academy-id 594425 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222341}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222341 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221425 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221177 :question/id #uuid "25d6bf3b-282e-5c8b-b665-923dd602849d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21669"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "785f39df-2c61-53b8-9293-093915b24356" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391364}] :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to a Second-Order ODE Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"} :step/id #uuid "5277b709-6b41-5099-b1a2-e16d03ed909a" :step/math-academy-id 594428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222529 :tutorial/content "We now apply the update rules $\\Delta y = y' \\cdot \\Delta x$ and $\\Delta z = z' \\cdot \\Delta x$ to our system below starting at $x=1$ with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{'}=\\frac{y+z}{x}+2,\\, & z(1)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nWe compute $y'$ and $\\Delta y,$ $z'$ and $\\Delta z$ (using the values of $x$, $y,$ and $z,$ in the first row), and the values of $x_\\text{new},$ $y_\\text{new},$ and $z_\\text{new}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{'} & =\\frac{y+z}{x}+2=\\frac{-1+1}{1}+2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{'}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{'}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nAfter several steps, we obtain the following table of values.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y$ | $z$ | $z'$ | $\\Delta z$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac32$ | $-\\dfrac12$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $\\dfrac32$ |\n| $2$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $\\dfrac74$ | $\\dfrac72$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\dfrac52$ | $\\dfrac94$ |  |  | $\\dfrac{11}2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac94$ and $y'\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) = z\\left(\\dfrac52\\right) \\approx \\dfrac{11}2.$ \n\nNotice that this approach of splitting a second-order ODE into a system of first-order ODEs is not specific to Euler's method. Indeed, we could apply any numerical method component-wise to approximate the solution to a second-order ODE once split into a system of first-order ODEs!" :tutorial/id #uuid "23470bd2-647f-5c35-b30d-10e88b4b7c3c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14856 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "2035b256-5c13-5188-9464-cbf223c96e9d" :step/math-academy-id 606510 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222339}}] :activity/title "Euler's Method for Second-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186221479}]}
